-- Two independent loan relationships in one signature - Analysis-Borrows.md §4.2 and §8.2.
--
-- Every signature in `lib/` has exactly one, which is why §4.8 measures the library as needing no
-- labels at all: a label exists to *split* a signature's group, never to name it. What splitting
-- one buys is here, and it is the difference between `keepsBoth` and `keepsLeft`.
--
-- **The group is written in the type**, which is where a group is written in every other position,
-- and nothing about it appears in the name area: that area is the binding convention and the name,
-- and it is kept clear so that argument patterns can have it. `return` is the one marker left there
-- and is allowed to be one because it is a keyword, so it can never be mistaken for a pattern;
-- §8.3 is its migration.
--
-- **`&x: T` and `x: &T` are one parameter and one type.** They were two - same effect, same ABI,
-- different FunTypes, so a function value written one way could not be passed where the other was
-- expected - and an exclusive reference in a parameter's type is now folded into the convention.
-- The group survives the fold, so `&a: l'Cell` and `a: &l'Cell` are the same declaration and this
-- fixture writes the first. A *shared* `'T` does not fold, and the reason is in bindingType: there
-- is no convention for a shared borrow that is not also pass-by-value, and pass-by-value on a
-- scalar is a copy.
--
-- **A reference type says the storage is somebody else's**, which is why an argument that was
-- copied and stored locally can never carry a tick. `x: Int` is a copy in a register; `x: 'Int` is
-- a reference to the caller's Int. That is what makes a reference parameter eligible to root a
-- result at all, and it is a fact about *ownership*.
--
-- **Whether the loan outlives the call is a second fact, and the result decides it.**
-- `fn mutate(f: &Flags) -> Int` takes a reference to the caller's storage and its loan still ends
-- with the call, because there is no loan in the result for it to outlive into - see
-- Contain.Borrow.yana, which writes through `f` after that call. So an unlabelled reference
-- parameter joins the anonymous group exactly when the result carries a loan, and every such
-- parameter joins the *same* group rather than one each: §4.8 rule 1, and what a conservative
-- `return` group already did. A label is what splits it.

data Cell {value: Int}

-- One group, with two parameters in it. A caller must hold *both* arguments borrowed until the
-- result's last use, because the body may have returned either - which is what a conservative
-- `return` group already does, and is why §4.8 rule 1 makes an unlabelled marker mean one group
-- rather than one group per parameter.
fn keepsBoth(&a: g'Cell, &b: g'Cell) -> &g'Cell = if a.value > b.value then a else b

-- The same signature in the spelling the whole library still uses. `return` is the one loan marker
-- left in the name area, and it stays there because it is a *keyword*: it cannot collide with an
-- argument pattern, which is what the rest of that area is being kept free for. It means §4.8 rule
-- 1's anonymous group, and §8.3 is its migration.
fn keepsBothMarked(return &a: Cell, return &b: Cell) -> &Cell = if a.value > b.value then a else b

-- Two groups. The result is in `l` alone, so a caller holds `a` borrowed until the result's last
-- use and is free to write through `b` immediately - which is what `main` does below.
fn keepsLeft(&a: l'Cell, &b: r'Cell) -> &l'Cell = a

-- The same relationship on the shared side, and with the result in the *second* parameter's group,
-- so that nothing here depends on a group's members being in order or on the first one winning.
fn keepsRight(left: l'Cell, right: r'Cell) -> r'Cell = right

-- And through a function value, which is what having the group on the *type* is for. A caller
-- reaching a function this way has only the type to read, so a contract that survived a direct call
-- and evaporated here would be worse than no contract - §8.4's premise, and the reason FunArg
-- carries the group rather than the declaration alone. `second` is writable inside `viaValue`
-- exactly as it is at a direct call.
fn viaValue(f: (&a: l'Cell, &b: r'Cell) -> &l'Cell) -> Int:
    let &first = Cell {value: 3}
    let &second = Cell {value: 4}
    let &picked = f(first, second)
    second.value = 7
    return picked.value + second.value

fn main() -> Int:
    let &first = Cell {value: 3}
    let &second = Cell {value: 4}

    -- `picked` is a loan of `first` and of nothing else, so `second` is writable while it lives.
    let &picked = keepsLeft(first, second)
    second.value = 7
    picked.value = picked.value + second.value

    let &third = Cell {value: 1}
    let &fourth = Cell {value: 2}

    -- And here the free one is the *first* argument, which one group would have kept borrowed.
    let chosen = keepsRight(third, fourth)
    third.value = 9

    -- And the `return` spelling reaches the same contract: one group, both arguments in it.
    let &fifth = Cell {value: 6}
    let &sixth = Cell {value: 8}
    let &larger = keepsBothMarked(fifth, sixth)

    return picked.value + chosen.value + third.value + larger.value + viaValue(keepsLeft)
